  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 256 AM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\n*** HOT START TO THE WEEK, THEN TURNING COOLER ***\n\nThe unseasonably hot weather will continue on Sunday as ridging \nrestrengthens behind the Saturday night shortwave. In fact, \nsubsidence in its wake should limit the chances for pop up afternoon \nstorms, though this also suggests the airmass should be slightly \nless humid compared to today and tomorrow. Even so, high \ntemperatures are slated to reach the mid 90s across parts of the \narea (highest southwest), which is contributing to 20-50% \nprobabilities for Major HeatRisk.\n\nAt long last, a shift to more seasonable conditions is progged to \nfollow the passage of a cool front accompanying a more potent upper \nlevel trough late Monday into Tuesday. Monday may remain quite \ntoasty - particularly across southern Kentucky where deterministic \nNBM forecast highs are in the low-mid 90s - depending on when the \nfront arrives, but by Tuesday a cooler and drier airmass should\nbe pouring into the region from the north. In addition, the \ndeterministic models (GFS, ECMWF, CMC) suggest this trough \nlingers over the Great Lakes/Northeast through at least late \nnext week to perpetuate continued northwest flow/cool advection.\nThe Low Resolution Ensemble Forecast (LREF) gives a 55-80% \n(70-85%) probability high temperatures don't climb out of the \n70s by next Wednesday (Thursday) area-wide.",
